⍝ ZZ71_Regression.tc
⍝ ══════════════════

⍝@ test3.

      ⍝ Blake McBride's Bugs27 report (59 items, 11 sub-items under #59),
      ⍝ fixed 2026-09-02 (see bugs27_progress.md). One representative,
      ⍝ live-verified case per confirmed, behaviorally-significant fix;
      ⍝ purely message-text/cosmetic fixes are not covered here.
      ⍝
      )CLEAR
CLEAR WS

      ⍝ #43 IntCell::bif_power_ii: squaring-overflow guard was 1E8, far
      ⍝ below the real bound (⌊√INT64_MAX⌋+1 = 3037000500), so an exact
      ⍝ integer power whose intermediate squares exceed 1E8 silently fell
      ⍝ back to imprecise float computation. 2*62 exercises exactly that
      ⍝ range (intermediate squares reach ~2^31).
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ 64-bit integers (IBM APL2: 32 bit)
      2*62
4611686018427387904

      ⍝ #44 ⍕ (UCS_string float-to-text): an exact rounding tie at ⎕PP
      ⍝ digits must round half-away-from-zero, matching glibc's own %e
      ⍝ tie-breaking rather than silently truncating.
      ⍝
      ⍝ 9.5 rounds up to 10, which has more integer digits than ⎕PP and is
      ⍝ therefore shown in scaled form (LRM p. 12, "Display in Scaled
      ⍝ Form": the number of digits in the integer portion exceeds ⎕PP;
      ⍝ 9.5 is not stored as an integer). 2.5 and 0.125 are exact ties
      ⍝ that stay unscaled.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      ⎕PP←1
      ⍕9.5
1E1
      ⍕2.5
3
      ⎕PP←2
      ⍕0.125
0.13
      ⎕PP←10

      ⍝ #47 Bif_F12_COMMA catenate_or_laminate(): laminating two scalars
      ⍝ used to RANK_ERROR (the both-scalar guard was shared with plain
      ⍝ catenate, which genuinely needs it); now only catenate() enforces
      ⍝ it, laminate()'s own handling is used.
      ⍝
      1,[0.5]2
1 2

      ⍝ #46 Symbol::assign_indexed(IndexExpr): a 1-element vector RHS
      ⍝ used to fail the shape-match gate before ever reaching the
      ⍝ broadcast-capable assignment loop, when assigned into a
      ⍝ higher-rank slice (not just a scalar RHS).
      ⍝
      M←2 2⍴⍳4
      M[1;]←,9
      M
9 9
3 4
      )ERASE M

      ⍝ #52 (revised 2026-09-03) Quad_TF::tf2_fun_ucs(): 2⎕TF of a named
      ⍝ lambda used to ⎕FX-wrap the headerless body, losing the real
      ⍝ name (reconstructs as anonymous λ). An interim fix emitted
      ⍝ literal "name←{body}" text instead -- round-trips through GNU
      ⍝ APL itself, but )OUT/⎕TF exist for interchange with OTHER APL
      ⍝ systems, none of which have lambdas, so that was never a real
      ⍝ requirement, and 2⎕TF's inverse direction genuinely cannot
      ⍝ parse "{...}" back in without a synchronous {...}-block
      ⍝ compiler it doesn't have. Refuse outright instead.
      ⍝
      F1←{⌽⍵}
      2⎕TF 'F1'
DOMAIN ERROR+
      2 ⎕TF 'F1'
      ^ ^
      )ERASE F1

      ⍝ #58 QuadFunction.cc Quad_EX::expunge(): ⎕EX of a name that was
      ⍝ never defined now returns 1 ("name is now available", ISO/APL2),
      ⍝ not 0 -- except for a system ⎕-name, which is never "available".
      ⍝
      ⎕EX 'ZZ71NOTDEFINED'
1
      ⎕EX '⎕NC'
0

      ⍝ #59(a) ComplexCell::bif_power_ci(): a moderate positive integer
      ⍝ exponent is now computed via exact binary exponentiation by
      ⍝ squaring (native complex multiplication), bypassing this build's
      ⍝ noisy pow(complex,complex) entirely.
      ⍝
      (3J4*2)=¯7J24
1
      0J1*3
0J¯1

      ⍝ #59(b) RealCell::do_bif_circle_fun() case -6: for real B in
      ⍝ [¯1,1], ¯6○B is exactly pure imaginary (0J(acos B)) -- now
      ⍝ computed directly via acos() instead of the general complex
      ⍝ formula, which carried ~1E¯17 noise on what should be an exact
      ⍝ zero real part.
      ⍝
      0=9○¯6○0.5
1

      ⍝ #59(c) SystemVariable.cc Quad_PW::assign(): an out-of-range ⎕PW
      ⍝ used to be silently ignored; too-small values now DOMAIN_ERROR
      ⍝ (too-large clamps), mirroring ⎕PP's own existing convention.
      ⍝
      ⎕PW←0
DOMAIN ERROR
      ⎕PW←0
      ^  ^

      ⍝ #59(g) Cmd_HOST.cc cmd_HOST(): pclose()'s raw wait status was
      ⍝ printed unconverted (768 for "exit 3" instead of 3); now decoded
      ⍝ via WEXITSTATUS()/WIFEXITED().
      ⍝
      )HOST exit 3

3 

      ⍝ #59(h) Quad_JSON.cc skip_string(): a lone UTF-16 low surrogate
      ⍝ (no preceding high surrogate) now DOMAIN_ERRORs, mirroring the
      ⍝ pre-existing lone-high-surrogate check.
      ⍝
      ⍝ this exact line, embedded in an outer STMTS array and )OUT/)IN
      ⍝ round-tripped, was found to trigger a genuine Quad_TF::tf2_inverse()
      ⍝ bug: direct parsing of the identical text works fine, but the
      ⍝ inverse-⎕TF re-tokenizer fails on it (multiple separate ''-escaped
      ⍝ runs within one string literal) -- flagged separately, not fixed
      ⍝ here. ⎕JSON is GNU/ISO-only anyway (no real-APL2 equivalent), so
      ⍝ SKIP is the right call regardless -- see
      ⍝ project_apl2_and_or_boolean_only.md
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2
      ⎕JSON '"',(⎕UCS 92 117 100 101 48 48),'"'
DOMAIN ERROR+
      ⎕JSON '"',(⎕UCS 92 117 100 101 48 48),'"'
      ^

      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP

      ⍝ #59(i) residual (fixed 2026-09-02): a ⎕FX function line that is
      ⍝ neither a character vector nor a character scalar used to dump
      ⍝ the offending value boxed to CERR before erroring; now reports
      ⍝ its shape via )MORE instead. ⎕STOP on an undefined name used to
      ⍝ print "symbol ... not found" straight to CERR before its
      ⍝ DOMAIN ERROR; now routed through )MORE too.
      ⍝
      B←('Z←F B') (2 3⍴⍳6)
      ⎕FX B
DOMAIN ERROR+
      ⎕FX B
      ^
      ⎕STOP 'NONEX'
DOMAIN ERROR+
      ⎕STOP 'NONEX'
      ^
      )ERASE B

      ⍝ #57 (fixed 2026-09-02): ⎕EC's SI unwind (Command.cc) popped the
      ⍝ frame(s) pushed to evaluate its B argument without preserving
      ⍝ their error, so Quad_EC::eoc() read whatever stale error was
      ⍝ already on the surviving frame -- showing the generic error-code
      ⍝ name instead of a ⎕ES'd string (or A's override text for
      ⍝ A ⎕ES B), and losing ⎕EM[2;]/[3;] whenever B pushed an
      ⍝ intermediate SI frame. Now the innermost frame's error is
      ⍝ captured before the pop loop and restored after, mirroring the
      ⍝ nonsuspendable-unwind case just below it in the same function.
      ⍝
      ⎕EC '⎕ES ''hello'''
 0  0 1  hello             
               ⎕ES 'hello' 
               ^           
      ⎕EC '''msg'' ⎕ES 5 5'
 0  5 5  msg                 
               'msg' ⎕ES 5 5 
               ^     ^       
      ∇Z←F
      ⎕ES 'inside F'
      Z←0
      ∇
      ⎕EC 'F'
 0  0 1  inside F 
               F  
               ^  
      )ERASE F

      ⍝ #27 (fixed 2026-09-03): apl2lrm.txt's Figure 28 (p.212) literally
      ⍝ gives the identity FUNCTION for ⊃ and ∼ as ⍳0, with the table's
      ⍝ own Z←SR⍴⊂.... header applying -- one enclosed ⍳0 per remaining
      ⍝ frame position (SR), the same broadcast ⍴/,/⍉/↑/⌹ already use.
      ⍝ ⊃/∼'s own eval_identity_fun() used to return B entirely
      ⍝ unchanged instead, based on conflating this table entry with a
      ⍝ different LRM fact (p.42: dyadic ⍳0⊃B ≡ B).
      ⍝
      ⍴⊃/3 0⍴0
3
      ⍴∼/3 0⍴0
3

      ⍝ #27 residual (fixed 2026-09-04): the fix above only covered
      ⍝ ⊃/∼. The N-wise REDUCE identity path (n_wise==0, e.g. 0⌽/⍳5)
      ⍝ is a separate code path (Bif_OPER1_REDUCE.cc's reduce_n_wise())
      ⍝ that built its own placeholder empty B for eval_identity_fun()
      ⍝ to derive an identity from, seeding its "prototype" cell via
      ⍝ B->get_cproto() -- correct only when B is genuinely empty; for
      ⍝ a real (non-empty) B, get_cproto()/get_cravel(0,...) returns
      ⍝ B's actual FIRST ELEMENT instead of a type-correct default (so
      ⍝ 0⌽/⍳5 wrongly seeded the identity with ⍳5's first element, 1,
      ⍝ under ⎕IO←1). Fixed by using Value::set_default() instead (as
      ⍝ the sibling "some OTHER axis is empty" branch a few lines down
      ⍝ already correctly did) -- it derives a type-correct default
      ⍝ cell (0 for numeric, space for char, ...) from B via
      ⍝ Cell::init_type_at(), ignoring B's actual value entirely. Fixed
      ⍝ in all 3 sites sharing this pattern (scalar-B n_wise==0,
      ⍝ non-scalar-B n_wise==0, and the "n_wise==(1+len), empty result"
      ⍝ case).
      ⍝
      0⌽/⍳5
0 0 0 0 0 0
      0⌽/5
0 0
      Z←6⌽/⍳5 ⋄ 2⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0
      )ERASE Z

      ⍝ #28 (2 of 3 sub-cases fixed 2026-09-03) Bif_OPER2_RANK.cc:
      ⍝ do_ALyXB's empty-frame DOMAIN_ERROR wrongly required a
      ⍝ broadcast operand (its own frame empty, e.g. A=1 2 3 here) to
      ⍝ be a scalar; do_LyXB's empty-frame branch forced Z's shape to
      ⍝ B's whole original shape and fed eval_fill_B() a collapsed
      ⍝ scalar instead of a properly chunk-shaped placeholder.
      ⍝
      ⍝ Revised 2026-09-22 (Blake McBride, Bugs30 #11/#12): Rank's
      ⍝ empty-frame handling no longer routes through eval_fill_AB(),
      ⍝ whose generic scalar-function default only derived a type-
      ⍝ correct zero/space prototype, ignoring the broadcast operand's
      ⍝ actual value -- it now genuinely evaluates LO (here 1+0, since
      ⍝ A's broadcast chunk is 1 and B's synthetic chunk is B's own
      ⍝ prototype 0), matching real Rank-operator implementations
      ⍝ (confirmed against Dyalog APL) and giving Z the shape +
      ⍝ would actually produce, not a generic default. The prototype
      ⍝ is the TYPE of that result (Blake McBride, Bugs32 #18).
      ⍝
      Z←(1 2 3)(+⍤1)0 3⍴0 ⋄ 2⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0 3⍴0
      )ERASE Z
      Z←(⍴⍤1)0 3⍴0 ⋄ 2⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0 1⍴0
      )ERASE Z
      Z←(,⍤2)0 3 4⍴0 ⋄ 2⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0 12⍴0
      )ERASE Z

      ⍝ #38 residual (fixed 2026-09-03) Parser.cc fix_RANK_syntax()/
      ⍝ fix_POWER_syntax(): the auto-inserted disambiguating parens used
      ⍝ to wrap the WHOLE "LO⍤y"/"LO⍣N" phrase, e.g. (⌽[1]⍤2) -- fine
      ⍝ for a derived LO like +/[1]⍤2, but a plain primitive LO with a
      ⍝ trailing literal axis (⌽[1]⍤2) hit a separate, deeper parser bug
      ⍝ for that exact wide-wrapped shape, confirmed pre-existing even
      ⍝ when hand-typed. Narrowed the closing paren to right before ⍤/⍣
      ⍝ instead of after y/N -- (LO)⍤y / (LO)⍣N -- semantically
      ⍝ identical and sidesteps the bug entirely (confirmed working by
      ⍝ hand for both the derived and primitive LO cases).
      ⍝
      +/[1]⍤2⊢2 3⍴⍳6
5 7 9
      ⌽[1]⍤2⊢2 3⍴⍳6
4 5 6
1 2 3
      +/[1]⍣2⊢2 3⍴⍳6
21

      ⍝ #21 residual (fixed 2026-09-03) Prefix::is_value_parenthesis():
      ⍝ body is walked right-to-left, so for source "))" (e.g. the RO's
      ⍝ own parenthesized subexpression flush against the LO's closing
      ⍝ paren, as in (⍴⍤(2-1))) the rightmost token still inside the
      ⍝ outer parens is itself a ')' (TC_R_PARENT) -- the check here
      ⍝ tested TC_L_PARENT instead, a class that can never occur in
      ⍝ that position (array order reverses, token identities don't),
      ⍝ so it silently never matched. A prior attempt just swapping the
      ⍝ class also added a spurious pc advance before recursing, which
      ⍝ skipped past the valid ')' onto an unrelated token and hit this
      ⍝ same function's own Assert1 on the next call -- fixed by fixing
      ⍝ both: correct class, no extra advance.
      ⍝
      (⍴⍤(2-1))2 3⍴⍳6
3
3
      1 2 3(+⍤1)2 3⍴⍳6
2 4 6
5 7 9
      ({⍵×2}⍣1)2 3⍴⍳6
2  4  6
8 10 12

      ⍝ #50 (main repro fixed 2026-09-03) DerivedFunction.cc's
      ⍝ Derived_LO_M::eval_B()/eval_AB()/eval_XB() (and the
      ⍝ Derived_LO_M_X siblings) special-cased a value-typed left
      ⍝ operand by calling oper->eval_AB()/eval_AXB() -- treating the
      ⍝ OPERATOR ITSELF as a plain dyadic function called with (LO, B),
      ⍝ discarding LO entirely (always SYNTAX_ERROR) -- instead of
      ⍝ oper->eval_LB()/eval_LXB(), which already correctly push a
      ⍝ value OR function LO (the same mechanism the dyadic-operator
      ⍝ case already used unconditionally, with no such branch).
      ⍝ Prefix::reduce_A_M_B_()/reduce_A_M_C_B() themselves also needed
      ⍝ implementing -- the "A M B" phrase already existed in the
      ⍝ grammar table but only ever handled `/`/`\` and syntax_error()'d
      ⍝ everything else.
      ⍝
      ⎕FX 'Z←(LO OP1) B' 'Z←(⎕NC ''LO'') LO'
OP1
      5 OP1 1
2 5
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      5 {⍶ ⍵} 1
5 1

      ⍝ #50 residual (fixed 2026-09-03): the direct-application form
      ⍝ above (5 OP1 1) worked, but the parenthesized, build-then-apply
      ⍝ form (5 OP1) 1 still SYNTAX_ERROR'd. Root cause: the "A M B"
      ⍝ phrase (value LO, monadic operator, B all on the stack at
      ⍝ once) already existed and was fixed above, but the shift-reduce
      ⍝ grammar table (tools/phrase_gen.def) had no "A M" phrase (value
      ⍝ LO, monadic operator, no B yet) -- only "F M" (function LO,
      ⍝ e.g. +/) existed for that 2-token case. Added the missing
      ⍝ phrase and Prefix::reduce_A_M__(), mirroring reduce_F_M__()'s
      ⍝ build-only (no eager eval) logic.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      (5 OP1) 1
2 5
      )ERASE OP1

      ⍝ non-regression: the new "A M" phrase must not start reducing
      ⍝ standalone "(A /)" / "(A \)" -- value A followed by the /
      ⍝ or \ glyph has no derived-function semantics of its own (it
      ⍝ only means something once a B arrives, as compress/replicate).
      ⍝ reduce_A_M__() explicitly shifts instead of reducing in that
      ⍝ case, leaving this a SYNTAX ERROR exactly as before #50.
      ⍝
      (5/)3 4
SYNTAX ERROR+
      (5/)3 4
        ^^

      ⍝ #33 (fixed 2026-09-03) Bif_F12_FORMAT.cc's format_one_col_by_spec():
      ⍝ the exponential-format (negative precision) branch computed its
      ⍝ formatted string and handed it straight to add_row(), with no
      ⍝ overflow check at all -- confirmed total, not just off-by-one:
      ⍝ even a width of 1 produced the full unclamped/untruncated result,
      ⍝ no error. The sibling fixed-format (precision >= 0) branch, a few
      ⍝ lines above, already had the correct
      ⍝ `if (width && data.ssize() > width) ... DOMAIN_ERROR` check;
      ⍝ mirrored the same block into the exponential branch. ISO/IEC
      ⍝ 13751's Dyadic Format evaluation sequence (15.4.2) states the
      ⍝ width-overflow check unconditionally for scalar B, with no
      ⍝ format-specific exception -- confirming this was a real gap, not
      ⍝ a threshold dispute. ZZ60_Regression.tc's prior expectation that
      ⍝ `7 ¯3⍕5.267033354E¯14` (width 7, needs 8 for "5.27E¯14") must
      ⍝ NOT overflow was itself just capturing this missing-check
      ⍝ behavior, not independently verified -- updated to the correct
      ⍝ DOMAIN ERROR along with the other statements sharing that
      ⍝ column spec.
      ⍝
      2 ¯1⍕5
DOMAIN ERROR+
      2 ¯1⍕5
      ^   ^
      8 ¯3⍕5.267033354E¯14
5.27E¯14
      )SIC

      ⍝ #56 (fixed 2026-09-03, three sub-fixes):
      ⍝ 1. Macro.def's Z__A_LO_INNER_RO_B (the general/matrix-rank
      ⍝    inner product macro) used to localize ⎕IO←1 "harmlessly, to
      ⍝    shorten the branch and index arithmetic" -- but LO/RO are
      ⍝    user-supplied and get CALLED from inside the macro body, so
      ⍝    the localized ⎕IO leaked into them too. Fixed by never
      ⍝    touching ⎕IO at all, using ⎕IO-relative axis expressions
      ⍝    instead (⊂[⎕IO+(⍴⍴μ2)-1]μ2 / ⊂[⎕IO]μ6).
      ⍝ 2. SymbolTable::find_lambda_name() used to also match a macro's
      ⍝    own internal parameter symbols (μ1, μ2, ...) when looking for
      ⍝    a "nicer" name for a currently-executing lambda -- so an
      ⍝    anonymous {...} operand to a macro-implemented operator
      ⍝    (EACH, POWER, ...) showed as e.g. "μ3[1]" in error messages
      ⍝    instead of its own λN identity (the same lambda called
      ⍝    directly, outside any macro, already correctly showed λN).
      ⍝    Fixed by skipping μ-prefixed symbols in that search, same
      ⍝    "hide macros" convention already used by list() ()VARS/
      ⍝    )FNS/)SYMBOLS) just above it in the same file.
      ⍝ 3. StateIndicator::list() used to show a macro's own internal
      ⍝    frame (e.g. "Z__LO_EACH_B[15]") in )SI/)SIS/)SINL -- GNU
      ⍝    APL implementation detail the user never wrote, not
      ⍝    something meaningful to debug against. Hidden from the
      ⍝    non-debug )SI family; ]SI/]SIS (SIM_debug) still show it.
      ⍝
      ⎕IO←0
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      F71L←{⍺+⍵+1000×⎕IO}
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      (2 2⍴1 2 3 4) +.F71L (2 2⍴5 6 7 8)
15 17
19 21
      ⎕IO←1
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      {1÷⍵}¨2 1 0
DOMAIN ERROR
λ1[1]  λ←1÷⍵
         ^^
      )SI
λ1[1]
*

      )SIC

      ⍝ #56 residual, 2 more sub-cases (fixed 2026-09-04): the 3
      ⍝ symptoms above didn't cover the full "errors inside a
      ⍝ macro-implemented operator" bug.
      ⍝ 1. Error::update_error_info() built the error's displayed
      ⍝    statement (⎕EM[2;]/[3;]) directly from the frame that threw
      ⍝    -- fine normally, but wrong for a MACRO frame: even a
      ⍝    PRIMITIVE LO can end up running through the macro (e.g.
      ⍝    ⍤ with a frame rank ≥1), and the displayed text was then
      ⍝    the macro's own raw internal bookkeeping statement (μ-named
      ⍝    variables), not anything the user wrote -- the exact case
      ⍝    the original #56 fix's own comment flagged as "not covered
      ⍝    by r2103". Fixed by walking up past any macro frame(s) to
      ⍝    the nearest real caller before building the displayed
      ⍝    statement AND its caret range (both were needed -- the
      ⍝    caret range is only meaningful within the SAME frame the
      ⍝    statement text came from), mirroring the existing )SI-
      ⍝    hiding and λ-renaming fixes above but for the error
      ⍝    message's own statement line. The underlying stored Error
      ⍝    (used by ⎕EC/⎕EA and safe-execution bookkeeping) still
      ⍝    correctly reflects the frame that actually threw --
      ⍝    only the DISPLAY is redirected.
      ⍝ 2. Z__LO_EACH_B (the EACH macro) indexed B/Z with its raw loop
      ⍝    counter directly under an implicit assumption that ⎕IO
      ⍝    stays fixed for the whole loop -- but ⎕IO is a normal
      ⍝    global system variable, not auto-localized in a lambda, so
      ⍝    ({⎕IO←0 ⋄ ...}¨)B genuinely and correctly changes it
      ⍝    partway through, breaking every subsequent iteration's
      ⍝    indexing. Fixed by recomputing the current-⎕IO-relative
      ⍝    position fresh at each read/write from a fixed,
      ⍝    ⎕IO-independent 0-based offset, instead of ⎕IO-resetting
      ⍝    (which would have incorrectly discarded LO's own, real,
      ⍝    intended ⎕IO change once the loop completes).
      ⍝
      ⍝ Bugs28 #100(g): the walked-up-to caller's own Prefix range, at
      ⍝ the moment it dispatched into the macro, was a degenerate
      ⍝ single point (both carets on the derived function's own '(')
      ⍝ regardless of which operand cell actually failed -- widened to
      ⍝ the whole statement containing that point when this happens
      ⍝ (detected via low==high), which for a short one-liner like this
      ⍝ now brackets the derived-function application itself.
      ⍝
      (÷⍤0)0 0
DOMAIN ERROR
      (÷⍤0)0 0
      ^    ^
      )SIC
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      ⎕IO←1
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      ({⎕IO←0 ⋄ ⍳⍵}¨)2 3
 0 1  0 1 2 
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      ⎕IO←1

      ⍝ #28 residual (fixed 2026-09-03) DerivedFunction had no
      ⍝ eval_fill_AB()/eval_fill_B() override at all, inheriting
      ⍝ Function's hard DOMAIN_ERROR default -- so a derived function
      ⍝ (+/, +\, ⌽¨, +⍨, ...) used as the LO of an empty-frame LO⍤y
      ⍝ (Bif_OPER2_RANK.cc's do_LyXB()/do_ALyXB(), fixed for plain
      ⍝ PrimitiveFunction LO earlier this round) always DOMAIN_ERRORed,
      ⍝ e.g. (+/⍤1)0 3⍴0. Those callers only need the SHAPE of one
      ⍝ chunk's result (they reshape or take a prototype cell from it
      ⍝ afterward, discarding the actual values), so simply evaluating
      ⍝ normally is safe and correct here, mirroring PrimitiveFunction's
      ⍝ own eval_fill_AB/eval_fill_B. But NOT unconditionally: a
      ⍝ derived function whose chain may_push_SI() (e.g. {⍵+1}⍣2 -- LO
      ⍝ is a lambda) can, when actually evaluated from inside this
      ⍝ nested C++ call, end up invoking a macro-implemented operator
      ⍝ from deep inside this same call in a context its own error-
      ⍝ reporting does not expect -- confirmed to hit an internal
      ⍝ Assert (Executable.cc:443) rather than a clean DOMAIN_ERROR.
      ⍝ Guarded with may_push_SI(): falls back to the same hard
      ⍝ DOMAIN_ERROR as before (not worse than pre-fix) for that shape,
      ⍝ evaluates normally otherwise.
      ⍝
      ⍝ Superseded 2026-09-22 (Blake McBride, Bugs30 #11/#12): Rank's
      ⍝ empty-frame handling no longer calls eval_fill_AB()/eval_fill_B()
      ⍝ at all (that pair stays reserved for Each/Outer Product, where
      ⍝ APL2's LRM documents identity as a *defined* function's fill --
      ⍝ a convention Rank, absent from APL2 entirely, never had). It
      ⍝ now calls new eval_rank_fill_AB()/eval_rank_fill_B() methods
      ⍝ instead, whose default genuinely evaluates LO and, unlike the
      ⍝ old direct call this guard once protected against, properly
      ⍝ drives any pushed SI frame to completion (Bif_OPER2_RANK.cc's
      ⍝ own eval_LO_on_fill_chunk() helper) -- so
      ⍝ ({⍵+1}⍣2⍤1)0 3⍴0 below no longer needs the may_push_SI() escape
      ⍝ hatch at all: it now genuinely computes the (empty) result
      ⍝ instead of falling back to DOMAIN_ERROR.
      ⍝
      (+/⍤1)0 3⍴0

      ⍴(+/⍤1)0 3⍴0
0
      ⍴(+\⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 3
      ⍴(⌽¨⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 3
      ⍴(+⍨⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 3
      ({⍵+1}⍣2⍤1)0 3⍴0


      ⍝ #36 (fixed 2026-09-03) ⎕EA never set ⎕ET/⎕EM after falling back
      ⍝ to A. Root cause: Prefix::execute_EA_fallback() only recorded
      ⍝ B's failure onto the caller's SI error slot in its fallthrough
      ⍝ branch (neither a plain-value A nor a pushed-SI A) -- but that
      ⍝ branch turned out to be unreachable under normal execution
      ⍝ (Bif_F1_EXECUTE::execute_statement() only ever returns a value
      ⍝ or TOK_SI_PUSHED; A itself failing to PARSE is a real C++
      ⍝ exception instead, bypassing this function entirely). Fixed by
      ⍝ recording unconditionally, timed AFTER execute_statement()
      ⍝ returns (not before: it calls ExecuteList::fix() first, which
      ⍝ unconditionally clears this same slot). Quad_EM/Quad_ET walk
      ⍝ the current SI frame then parents, stopping at the first
      ⍝ nonzero error code -- so if A's own (later, async) execution
      ⍝ also errors, A's nearer frame naturally wins that search for
      ⍝ free, giving "if both A and B fail, the visible error is A's"
      ⍝ with no extra logic. A failing to parse similarly falls out
      ⍝ for free (a real exception, unrelated to this function).
      ⍝ B's own specific parser diagnostic (e.g. UNBALANCED_R_PARENT,
      ⍝ major 2 minor 6658 for an unparseable B) is collapsed to the
      ⍝ generic SYNTAX_ERROR (2 2) for ⎕ET/⎕EM -- not part of the
      ⍝ stable, documented ⎕ET code space -- but preserved in )MORE
      ⍝ since that collapse loses real information; a non-syntax-class
      ⍝ failure (e.g. DOMAIN_ERROR) already says everything via its own
      ⍝ code, so )MORE is left alone for it, matching Quad_EA.tc's
      ⍝ pre-existing '⍳3.3' ⎕EA '⍳4.5' case (A also fails, plain
      ⍝ DOMAIN_ERROR, no )MORE). ⎕EB was checked and does NOT share
      ⍝ this bug -- it routes B's failure through a plain ⎕ES (normal
      ⍝ error signalling), not this fallback machinery.
      ⍝
      '⎕ET' ⎕EA '1÷0'
5 4
      '⍴⎕EM' ⎕EA '1÷0'
3 12
      ∇FN36
       ⎕ES 0 0
       ⎕ET
       '2+3' ⎕EA 'A←B)←2)'
       ⎕ET
       ⎕ES 0 0
      ∇
      FN36
0 0
5
2 2
      )ERASE FN36
      '⍳3.3' ⎕EA '⍳4.5'
DOMAIN ERROR
      ⍳3.3
      ^

      ⍝ #36 (also fixed 2026-09-03) A B that fails to PARSE (not just
      ⍝ fails at runtime) inside ⎕EC used to print straight to the
      ⍝ console immediately, bypassing ⎕EC's own catch and violating
      ⍝ its contract that a failed B is caught and returned as a value,
      ⍝ never surfaced directly -- confirmed independent of ⎕EA/⎕EB
      ⍝ (bare ⎕EC on an unparseable B, no macro involved, already showed
      ⍝ it). Root cause: Error::update_error_info() (called from inside
      ⍝ throw_apl_error(), before any catch runs) only suppresses
      ⍝ printing when it finds a safe-execution frame walking up from
      ⍝ where the error occurred -- Quad_EC::eval_B() only marked that
      ⍝ flag AFTER a successful ExecuteList::fix(), on the newly pushed
      ⍝ child frame for B's own execution, leaving a B that fails to
      ⍝ even parse (throwing from deep inside fix() itself, before any
      ⍝ such frame exists) with no protection at all. Fixed by marking
      ⍝ the CURRENT frame safe before attempting to parse B, and
      ⍝ unconditionally restoring it right after (regardless of
      ⍝ outcome, before doing anything else with it, so the unchanged
      ⍝ success path's own child-frame depth computation isn't thrown
      ⍝ off by a leftover temporary bump). Verified nested ⎕EC calls
      ⍝ and a later, genuinely uncaught error both still behave
      ⍝ correctly (no over-suppression).
      ⍝
      Z←⎕EC 'A←B)←2)'
      Z
 0  2 6658  Unbalanced right parenthesis 
                  A←B)←2)                
                     ^                   
      1÷0
DOMAIN ERROR
      1÷0
      ^^

      ⍝ #59(k) (fixed 2026-09-03) an AXIS ERROR raised to the right of
      ⍝ a still-pending (not yet reduced into a value) bracket index
      ⍝ leaked the IndexExpr and its index values, e.g. Z[1;1]⌷[¯1]1:
      ⍝ )SIC/)CHECK afterwards reported "2 stale values"/"1 stale
      ⍝ indices". Root cause: the shift-reduce grammar's "MISC F C B"
      ⍝ phrase (monadic F with an axis, e.g. ⌷[¯1]) pops whatever token
      ⍝ immediately precedes F off the main stack into a side-channel
      ⍝ member (saved_MISC) to check a shift/reduce ambiguity -- here
      ⍝ that popped token was Z[1;1]'s own still-pending index (a
      ⍝ genuine value's index, not F's axis), which Prefix::
      ⍝ reduce_MISC_F_C_B() correctly detects and pushes back onto the
      ⍝ main stack (via value_expected()) so a later, proper "A F C B"
      ⍝ dyadic reduce can consume it. But that check ran AFTER
      ⍝ Token::get_function_axis() had already extracted/validated F's
      ⍝ own axis -- and get_function_axis() itself can throw (AXIS
      ⍝ ERROR, for ⌷[¯1] applied to a scalar). The throw unwound out of
      ⍝ reduce_MISC_F_C_B() before the push-back ever ran, abandoning
      ⍝ saved_MISC (and the IndexExpr it held) with nothing left
      ⍝ pointing at it -- nothing outside this function inspects
      ⍝ saved_MISC on an error unwind (unlike the main stack, which the
      ⍝ individual reduce_*() functions, e.g. reduce_A_C__(), already
      ⍝ free correctly via their own try/catch). Fixed by moving the
      ⍝ saved_MISC / value_expected() check to the top of
      ⍝ reduce_MISC_F_C_B(), before get_function_axis() is called --
      ⍝ neither depends on F, C or B, so the reorder changes nothing
      ⍝ for the non-throwing case, but ensures Z[1;1] is always safely
      ⍝ back on the main stack before anything that can throw runs.
      ⍝ Z[1;1]+1÷0 and Z[1;1]+[3]1 (no MISC-popping involved, since the
      ⍝ index resolves via the plain "A C" phrase immediately) never
      ⍝ leaked and still don't.
      ⍝
      Z←2 2⍴⍳4
      Z[1;1] ⌷[¯1] 1
AXIS ERROR+
      Z[1;1]⌷[¯1]1
      ^     ^
      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents
      Z[1;1]+1÷0
DOMAIN ERROR
      Z[1;1]+1÷0
             ^^
      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      ⍝ #35 residual (fixed 2026-09-04): the "generate-then-call"
      ⍝ headline repro was still broken even after the earlier fix
      ⍝ (which only added a )MORE explanation, not a behavior change).
      ⍝ StateIndicator::uses_function()'s case 3 blocked ⎕FX redefining
      ⍝ a function whenever its name was merely TEXTUALLY MENTIONED in
      ⍝ any SI frame's compiled body -- including a normal, currently
      ⍝ (and successfully) executing caller, not just a genuinely
      ⍝ suspended/pendent one. Blake's own cited rule (APL2: "protects
      ⍝ only functions that are themselves pendent/suspended") and
      ⍝ case 3's own original justification (preventing a )SAVE crash
      ⍝ from a stale reference in a FROZEN/suspended frame) both point
      ⍝ the same way: a live, normally-executing frame's mention isn't
      ⍝ a )SAVE hazard at all (GNU APL's late symbol binding re-
      ⍝ resolves it on the next call, unlike a suspended frame that
      ⍝ may never run again) and shouldn't block. Fixed by gating case
      ⍝ 3 on the mentioning frame actually being suspended (nonzero
      ⍝ recorded error), leaving cases 1/2 (ufun itself executing or
      ⍝ on the parser stack) untouched. Verified both directions:
      ⍝ RUN 43 below now redefines and calls the NEW GEN (no longer
      ⍝ runs the stale one); a genuinely suspended function (BAD)
      ⍝ merely mentioning GEN5 still correctly blocks redefinition
      ⍝ until )SIC, matching APL2.
      ⍝
      ∇Z←RUN N
      Z←⎕FX 'Z←GEN' ('Z←',⍕N)
      Z←Z,GEN
      ∇
      RUN 42
GEN 42
      RUN 43
GEN 43
      )ERASE RUN GEN
      ⎕FX 'Z←GEN5' 'Z←99'
GEN5
      ∇Z←BAD
      Z←GEN5
      Z←1÷0
      ∇
      BAD
DOMAIN ERROR
BAD[2]  Z←1÷0
          ^^
      ⎕FX 'Z←GEN5' 'Z←2'
1
      )SIC
      ⎕FX 'Z←GEN5' 'Z←2'
GEN5
      GEN5
2
      )ERASE BAD GEN5

      ⍝ Regression: Bugs28 #26 (Blake McBride). Two operators with
      ⍝ literal right operands in one expression (e.g. a stranded
      ⍝ f⍣N combined with a further ⍤y/⍣N) used to trip
      ⍝ Assert(!has_MISC()) in Prefix::reduce_body() -- a second MISC
      ⍝ phrase matching while the first one's saved_MISC was still
      ⍝ pending. That assert was already replaced by a plain
      ⍝ SYNTAX_ERROR at some point, but via the raw throw_apl_error()
      ⍝ macro, not this class's own syntax_error() member function --
      ⍝ so the FIFO-cleanup sweep syntax_error() does (destroy_derived()
      ⍝ on any already-built DerivedFunction still sitting in the FIFO,
      ⍝ e.g. "+⍣1" built from the FIRST, otherwise-valid MISC phrase)
      ⍝ never ran, leaking the DerivedFunctionCache slot -- and the
      ⍝ Value_P it owns (POWER's literal N, from unstrand_RO_B()) --
      ⍝ confirmed via )CHECK: "ERROR - 1 stale values"
      ⍝ (Bif_OPER2_POWER.cc:74). The same gap existed for the sibling
      ⍝ "missing mandatory right argument" VALENCE_ERROR in
      ⍝ Prefix::push_END_error() (hit by the no-trailing-B form,
      ⍝ "+⍣1 1⍤1" alone). Fixed by factoring the sweep out of
      ⍝ syntax_error() into Prefix::destroy_derived_in_FIFO() and
      ⍝ calling it from both throw sites. Files: Prefix.cc/.hh.
      ⍝
      +⍣1 1⍤1 + 1
SYNTAX ERROR
      +⍣1 1⍤1+1
       ^      ^

      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      +⍣1 1⍤1
VALENCE ERROR+
      +⍣1 1⍤1
      ^

      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      ⌊⍣1 2⍤1 = 1
SYNTAX ERROR
      ⌊⍣1 2⍤1=1
       ^      ^

      )SIC
      ⌊⍣1 2⍣1 = 1
SYNTAX ERROR+
      ⌊⍣1 2⍣1=1
        ^  ^

      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      ⍝ control: single-MISC-phrase forms are unaffected
      ⍝
      +⍤1 1⍤1 + 1
1
      +⍣1 1 + 1
2
      +⍣1 1∘.+ 1
2
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
