      ⍝ Regression: Bugs28 #90 (Blake McBride). Assigning the (missing)
      ⍝ result of a chain ending in a VOID-returning defined function
      ⍝ (e.g. X←⍴F1, where F returns no value) used to reach
      ⍝ Prefix::reduce_V_ASS_F_() -- the "V ← {lambda}" named-lambda-
      ⍝ assignment phrase -- once "F1" reduced away, leaving "X ← ⍴"
      ⍝ looking like "V ← {a lambda}" one token later. Since ⍴ (the F
      ⍝ here) is not actually a lambda, this raised a raw, unexplained
      ⍝ SYNTAX ERROR via the SYNTAX_ERROR macro directly, bypassing this
      ⍝ class's own syntax_error() member function and its "was this
      ⍝ caused by a function producing no value?" check -- so the
      ⍝ correct VALUE ERROR (which the same expression WITHOUT the
      ⍝ assignment, bare ⍴F1, already correctly gives) was lost. Fixed
      ⍝ by calling syntax_error(LOC) instead of the raw macro.
      ⍝
      ⎕FX 'ZZ90F B' 'Q←B'
ZZ90F

      ⍴ZZ90F 1
VALUE ERROR+
      ⍴ZZ90F 1
       ^

      )MORE
No assignment to result variable Z in e.g. ∇Z←... ?
      )SIC

      X←⍴ZZ90F 1
VALUE ERROR+
      X←⍴ZZ90F 1
         ^

      )MORE
No assignment to result variable Z in e.g. ∇Z←... ?
      )SIC

      ⎕←⍴ZZ90F 1
VALUE ERROR+
      ⎕←⍴ZZ90F 1
         ^

      )MORE
No assignment to result variable Z in e.g. ∇Z←... ?
      )SIC

      X←⍴⍎'  '
VALUE ERROR+
      X←⍴⍎'  '
         ^

      )MORE
No assignment to result variable Z in e.g. ∇Z←... ?
      )SIC

      ⍝ control: X←F1 (no function between ← and F) already correctly
      ⍝ gave VALUE ERROR before this fix and still does; a genuine named-
      ⍝ lambda assignment is unaffected either way
      ⍝
      X←ZZ90F 1
VALUE ERROR+
      X←ZZ90F 1
        ^
      )SIC

      f←{⍵+1}
      f 5
6
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      (g←{⍵}) 5
5
      1+(h←{⍵})5
6

      )ERASE ZZ90F f g h

      ⍝ Regression: Bugs28 #60 (Blake McBride). A parenthesized
      ⍝ replicate/expand inside a selective-specification target
      ⍝ (e.g. (1↑(2/A))←7) was SYNTAX ERROR, while the same thing
      ⍝ without the inner (purely grouping, semantically meaningless)
      ⍝ parens -- (1↑2/A)←7 -- worked fine. This parser shifts right-
      ⍝ to-left, so by the time A (the underlying variable) is reached,
      ⍝ the extra ')' from the inner group is ALREADY on the stack on
      ⍝ top of the one closing the whole target -- and
      ⍝ Prefix::reduce_V_RPAR_ASS_B() (phrase "V RPAR ASS B", the code
      ⍝ that resolves A to an lvalue) only ever matches when EXACTLY
      ⍝ one ')' separates A from the real "← B". With two (or more)
      ⍝ ')' in the way, that phrase never fires, A is left as a bare,
      ⍝ unresolved symbol, and once "/" tries to use it as an argument
      ⍝ the parser -- unable to match "A F B" (which needs a VALUE, not
      ⍝ a SYMBOL) -- silently misreads "2/" alone as a deferred
      ⍝ derived-function build instead, and the whole statement gets
      ⍝ stuck in an unexplained SYNTAX ERROR. Fixed in
      ⍝ Prefix::push_Symbol(): when at least two ')' are already on the
      ⍝ stack and the next token isn't itself a symbol (ruling out a
      ⍝ vector assignment "(T U V)←..." still being collected, and the
      ⍝ sibling "(F V)←..." form already handled by the generic "F V"
      ⍝ phrase), resolve the symbol to its lvalue right away -- the
      ⍝ extra ')' then strip away transparently via ordinary "(value)"
      ⍝ grouping, and the already-generic "A ASS B" phrase
      ⍝ (Prefix::reduce_A_ASS_B_()) finishes the write, exactly as it
      ⍝ already does for e.g. (⊃E)[]←0.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      ZZ60A←10 20 30 40 50
      (1↑2/ZZ60A)←7
      ZZ60A
7 20 30 40 50

      ⍝ same target, now with the inner grouping parens that used to
      ⍝ break it -- must give the identical result
      ⍝
      ZZ60A←10 20 30 40 50
      (1↑(2/ZZ60A))←7
      ZZ60A
7 20 30 40 50

      ⍝ control: arbitrarily deeper (here: 3-level) nesting works too,
      ⍝ not just the specific 2-')' case found by Blake
      ⍝
      ZZ60A←10 20 30 40 50
      ((1↑(2/ZZ60A)))←7
      ZZ60A
7 20 30 40 50

      ⍝ control: vector assignment (a sibling "V RPAR ASS B" case that
      ⍝ must NOT be treated as a selective specification) is unaffected
      ⍝
      (ZZ60T ZZ60U)←5 6
      ZZ60T
5
      ZZ60U
6

      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      )ERASE ZZ60A ZZ60T ZZ60U

      ⍝ Blake McBride's Bugs30 #11/#12: (F⍤k)B and A∘.F B with a *defined*
      ⍝ F, on an empty frame. See Bif_OPER2_RANK.cc (eval_rank_fill_AB()/
      ⍝ eval_rank_fill_B(), eval_LO_on_fill_chunk()) for the #11 fix.
      ⍝
      ⍝ #11 (⍤, not part of APL2 at all -- ISO 13751 9.3.4/9.3.5 never
      ⍝ addresses the empty-frame case either) was a real bug: a defined
      ⍝ LO used to get APL2's Each/Outer-Product-only "identity" fill
      ⍝ (LRM Figure 20), simply returning the chunk argument unevaluated,
      ⍝ e.g. ({⍴⍵}⍤1)0 3⍴0 wrongly gave shape 0 3 (B's own chunk shape)
      ⍝ instead of 0 1 (the shape ⍴ actually produces on one chunk),
      ⍝ disagreeing with the primitive control (⍴⍤1), which already
      ⍝ computed the real shape. LO is now genuinely evaluated on a
      ⍝ synthetic chunk instead, matching real Rank-operator
      ⍝ implementations -- confirmed against both Dyalog APL and IBM
      ⍝ APL2 itself (⍤ is a widely adopted extension there too).
      ⍝
      ⍴(⍴⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 1
      ⍴({⍴⍵}⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 1
      ⍴({2↑⍵}⍤1)1 3⍴0
1 2
      ⍴({2↑⍵}⍤1)0 3⍴0
0 2
      ⍴({⍵,⍵}⍤1)1 3⍴5
1 6
      ⍴({⍵,⍵}⍤1)0 3⍴5
0 6

      ⍝ #12 (∘., genuinely part of APL2): Blake's report expected the
      ⍝ same treatment as #11 (genuine evaluation of a defined RO), but
      ⍝ ∘. IS covered by APL2's LRM (Figure 20), which documents identity
      ⍝ -- not evaluation -- as a defined operation's fill, and real APL2
      ⍝ confirmed that directly (≡Z, ⍴↑Z, and 2⎕TF all matched GNU APL's
      ⍝ existing, UNCHANGED output on both cases below). So unlike #11,
      ⍝ this is NOT a bug: these are regression controls confirming that
      ⍝ UserFunction::eval_fill_AB()'s plain identity is already correct
      ⍝ here and must stay that way -- genuinely evaluating a defined RO
      ⍝ (as #11 now does for LO) would make this WRONG relative to real
      ⍝ APL2, not right.
      ⍝
      Z←⍬∘.,1 2 3 ⋄ ≡Z ⋄ ⍴↑Z ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
2
2
Z←0 3⍴(⊂2⍴0 0)
      )ERASE Z
      Z←⍬∘.{⍺,⍵}1 2 3 ⋄ ≡Z ⋄ ⍴↑Z ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
1

Z←0 3⍴0
      )ERASE Z
      Z←3∘.,0⍴⊂1 2 ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴(⊂3⍴0 0 0)
      )ERASE Z
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ lambdas are a GNU APL extension
      Z←3∘.{⍺,⍵}0⍴⊂1 2 ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴(⊂2⍴0 0)
      )ERASE Z

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #29): a selective specification
      ⍝ rejected by one of the r2121 selectivity guards -- Prefix.cc's
      ⍝ reduce_MISC_F_B_()/reduce_A_F_B_() (the "F does not genuinely
      ⍝ select/overtake B" checks) and Bif_OPER1_EACH.cc's matching
      ⍝ monadic/dyadic guards -- raised SYNTAX_ERROR/let the non-
      ⍝ selecting function run to its natural VALENCE_ERROR without ever
      ⍝ calling Value::neutralize_lval_cells() on the rejected lvalue/
      ⍝ cellrefs array first. Bugs30 #28's fix only covered the
      ⍝ LENGTH_ERROR branches *inside* assign_cellrefs() itself, one step
      ⍝ later -- these guards run BEFORE assign_cellrefs() is ever
      ⍝ reached, so the array (and its live LvalCells) stayed on this
      ⍝ (unpopped, until )SIC) Prefix's operand stack, a leak (not a
      ⍝ use-after-free) that )CHECK's "value has N Lval Cells" warning
      ⍝ then reported. Every one of these must leave )CHECK clean.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      V←1 2 3
      (+¨V)←9
SYNTAX ERROR
      (+¨V)←9
       ^    ^
      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

